    "https://careers.wlu.ca/job/Waterloo-Tenure-Track-Position-Communication-Studies-%28Environmental-Communication%29-ON/1291550347/": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Position",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Position - Communication Studies (Environmental Communication) Job Details | Wilfrid Laurier University Skip to main content Home Page About Us Why Join Our Team How to apply Career Opportunities Academic Positions Staff and Management Positions View All Positions Join Talent Community Search by Keyword Search by Location Clear Home Page About Us Why Join Our Team How to apply Career Opportunities Academic Positions Staff and Management Positions View All Positions Join Talent Community Sign Into My Profile Search by Keyword Search by Location Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Tenure-Track Position - Communication Studies (Environmental Communication) Apply now » Date: Sep 15, 2026 Location: Waterloo, CA Company: Wilfrid Laurier University Faculty/Academic Area: Faculty of Arts Department : Communication Studies Campus: Waterloo Employee Group: WLUFA Requisition ID: 11859 Tenure-Track Position – Communication Studies (Environmental Communication) The Department/Program of Communication Studies at Wilfrid Laurier University invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level, commencing July 1, 2027. This position is subject to budgetary approval. Anticipated Salary Range: $92K to $112K. Actual salary to be commensurate with experience. Communication Studies is a leading program in the critical study of communication and media in Canada. We study the intersection of language, media and culture from an interdisciplinary perspective and train students in the histories, theories and methodologies of Communication Studies. The department also offers a Combined BA in Cultural Studies, and an MA in Communication Studies. With an average of nearly 700 majors each year, Communication Studies is one of the largest programs in the Faculty of Arts and in 2026 we celebrate our 25th anniversary. We seek to hire a critical communication studies scholar with demonstrated expertise in environmental communication. We encourage applicants whose research and teaching incorporates global and/or Indigenous studies perspectives. Applicants must have a completed PhD in Communication Studies or a cognate discipline by the start of the appointment. The preferred candidate will have a strong research record, as evidenced by publication in peer-reviewed sources, and a good record of teaching. The teaching workload for this position is four one-term courses, across all levels of the undergraduate and Master’s programs. Applications must include: A letter of application A curriculum vitae Teaching Dossier (should include copies of course evaluations, selected course outlines, and a brief reflection on teaching and the applicants’ teaching experience) Up to 2 selected publications The contact information of at least three academic referees (telephone and email). Only complete applications will be considered.  All applications should be addressed to: Peter Urquhart Chair, Communication Studies Faculty of Arts Wilfrid Laurier University Waterloo, Ontario, N2L 3C5 Email: purquhart@wlu.ca Deadline for applications is November 1, 2026 . Diversity and creating a culture of inclusion is a key pillar of Wilfrid Laurier University’s Strategic Academic Plan and is one of Laurier’s core values. Laurier is committed to increasing the diversity of faculty and staff and welcomes applications from qualified members of the equity-deserving groups. Indigenous candidates who would like to learn more about equity and inclusive programming at Laurier are welcome to contact the Office of Indigenous Initiatives at indigenous@wlu.ca. Candidates from other equity-deserving groups who would like to learn more about equity and inclusive programming at Laurier are welcomed to contact Equity and Accessibility at equity@wlu.ca . All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, as per Canadian immigration laws, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. To comply with the Government of Canada’s reporting requirements, the University is obligated to gather information about applicants’ status as either Permanent Residents of Canada or Canadian citizens. Applicants need not identify their country of origin or current citizenships, however, all applicants must include one of the following statements in their cover letter: Yes, I am a current citizen or permanent resident of Canada; No, I am not a current citizen or permanent resident of Canada Members of the designated groups must self-identify to be considered for employment equity. Candidates should self-identify, in confidence, to Dean Sofie Lachapelle, Dean of Arts ( foadeansoffice@wlu.ca ). Further information on the equity policy can be found at https://www.wlu.ca/about/governance/assets/resources/8.4-employment-equity.html . Applicants are encouraged to address any career interruptions or special circumstances that may have affected their record of research and teaching, in accordance with SSHRC and NSERC definitions and guidelines. To obtain a copy of this job description in an accessible format, please contact Justine MacNeil (foadeansoffice@wlu.ca). The Faculty of Arts wishes to thank all applicants for their interest. All nominations and applications shall be reviewed and considered under a set of criteria established by the Search Committee and a short list of candidates shall be interviewed. Only those applicants selected for the short list will be contacted. We would like to acknowledge that Wilfrid Laurier University and its campuses are located on the shared traditional territory of the Neutral, Anishnaabe (Anish-nah-bay) and Haudenosaunee (Hoe-den-no-show-nee) peoples. This land is part of the Dish with One Spoon Treaty between the Haudenosaunee and Anishnaabe peoples and symbolizes the agreement to share, protect our resources and not to engage in conflict. From the Haldimand Proclamation of Oct. 25, 1784 this territory is described as: “six miles deep from each side of the river (Grand River) beginning at Lake Erie and extending in the proportion to the Head of said river, which them and their posterity are to enjoy forever.” The proclamation was signed by the British with their allies, the Six Nations, after the American Revolution. Despite being the largest reserve demographically in Canada, those nations now reside on less than five percent of this original territory. Wilfrid Laurier University endeavors to fill positions with qualified candidates who have a combination of education, experience, skills and abilities to successfully perform the duties of the position while demonstrating Laurier's Employee Success Factors. Equity, diversity and creating a culture of inclusion are part of Laurier’s core values and central to the Laurier Strategy. Laurier is committed to increasing the diversity of faculty and staff and welcomes applications from candidates who identify as Indigenous, racialized, having disabilities, and from persons of any minority sexual and gender identities. Indigenous candidates who would like to learn more about equity and inclusive programing at Laurier are welcomed to contact the Office of Indigenous Initiatives. Candidates from other equity deserving groups who would like to learn more about equity and inclusive programing at Laurier are welcomed to contact Equity & Accessibility. We have strived to make our application process accessible, however if you require any assistance applying for a position or would like this job posting in an alternative format, please contact Human Resources. Contact information can be found at careers.wlu.ca/content/How-to-apply/ Should you be interested in learning more about this opportunity, please visit www.wlu.ca/careers for additional information and the online application system. All applications must be submitted online. Please note, a CV and letter of introduction will be required in electronic form. Job Segment: Communications, Environmental Engineering, Developer, Web Design, Engineering, Marketing, Research, Technology, Creative Apply now » Find similar jobs: All jobs, Academic Positions Careers Home Frequently Searched Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © Copyright 2020 All Rights Reserved"
    },
    "https://careers.wlu.ca/job/Milton-Tenure-Track-Appointment-Assistant-Professor%2C-Business-Technology-Management-%28MiltonBrantford%29-QC/1291561447/": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Appointment",
      "description": "Tenure Track Appointment - Assistant Professor, Business Technology Management (Milton/Brantford) Job Details | Wilfrid Laurier University Skip to main content Home Page About Us Why Join Our Team How to apply Career Opportunities Academic Positions Staff and Management Positions View All Positions Join Talent Community Search by Keyword Search by Location Clear Home Page About Us Why Join Our Team How to apply Career Opportunities Academic Positions Staff and Management Positions View All Positions Join Talent Community Sign Into My Profile Search by Keyword Search by Location Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Tenure Track Appointment - Assistant Professor, Business Technology Management (Milton/Brantford) Apply now » Date: Sep 17, 2026 Location: Milton, CA Company: Wilfrid Laurier University Faculty/Academic Area: Lazaridis School of Bus. & Econ. Department : Lazaridis Business (BTM) Campus: Milton Employee Group: WLUFA Requisition ID: 11860 DO NOT CLICK THE “APPLY NOW” BUTTON. Please submit your application following the instructions below. The Lazaridis School of Business & Economics (Lazaridis School) at Wilfrid Laurier University invites applications for one tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the Business Technology Management (BTM) program, commencing July 1, 2027 . The position may involve teaching at both the Brantford and Milton campuses and is subject to budgetary approval. Anticipated Salary Range: $110,000 to $160,000. The successful candidate’s salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Applicants should hold a PhD, or be near completion of a doctorate, in Management with a specialization in Information Systems (MIS), technology and innovation management, or a related management discipline such as strategic management, organizational theory, operations management, or data analytics. Possessing significant technical experience or holding a relevant undergraduate or master’s degree in computer science or engineering will be considered an asset. The successful candidate will demonstrate exceptional research potential, including a demonstrated ability to publish in top-tier journals in the field and to develop and sustain a highly productive research agenda. Priority will be given to applicants who can effectively apply management theories to high-technology industries and engage business students in technology-focused courses. Preference will be given to those who have experience teaching cases and using other innovative experiential learning methods in the classroom. The successful candidate will be expected to strengthen the BTM area’s teaching expertise and capabilities and to collaborate effectively with colleagues in the ongoing development of curricula and teaching materials. The successful candidate will also be expected to make exceptional service contributions to the BTM area and the Lazaridis School of Business & Economics. This includes an established capacity to contribute to the development of experiential learning opportunities for BTM students and to support the program through accreditation processes. The Lazaridis School of Business & Economics has more than 100 full-time faculty members and over 5,000 undergraduate and graduate students across campuses in Waterloo, Brantford, and Milton, as well as a location in Toronto, Ontario. Committed to excellence in management education, the School offers a range of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including the Honours BBA, MBA, MFin, and PhD. Laurier’s undergraduate and graduate business programs are AACSB accredited. For more information, please visit http://www.wlu.ca/lazaridisschool Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, and research statement, and arrange for at least three academic referees to submit letters of reference to btmptac@wlu.ca by the application deadline. Applicants may also include teaching evaluations or other evidence of teaching effectiveness. Only complete applications will be considered. All applications must include Position Number BTM TT 11860 and should be addressed to: Professor Ling Chu Associate Dean of Business: Faculty Development & Research Lazaridis School of Business & Economics Wilfrid Laurier University Waterloo, Ontario, N2L 3C5 Email: btmptac@wlu.ca Application deadline: November 1, 2026. Wilfrid Laurier University is committed to equity and values diversity. Laurier welcomes applications from qualified candidates who identify as Indigenous, racialized, or as persons with disabilities, as well as qualified candidates of all sexual orientations and gender identities. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration law, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority. To comply with Government of Canada reporting requirements, the University is required to collect information regarding applicants’ status as Canadian citizens or permanent residents. Applicants are not required to identify their country of origin or current citizenship; however, all applicants must include one of the following statements in their cover letter: Yes, I am currently a Canadian citizen or permanent resident of Canada; No, I am not currently a Canadian citizen or permanent resident of Canada. Members of designated groups must self-identify to be considered under employment equity provisions. Candidates may self-identify, in confidence, to Michael Lisetto-Smith, Senior Administrative Officer (mlisettosmith@wlu.ca). Further information on the University’s equity policy can be found here. Applicants are encouraged to describe any career interruptions or special circumstances that may have affected their research or teaching record, in accordance with SSHRC and NSERC definitions and guidelines. To request this job description in an accessible format, please contact Helen Kaluzny (hkaluzny@wlu.ca). The Lazaridis School of Business & Economics thanks all applicants for their interest. All nominations and applications will be reviewed and assessed according to criteria established by the Search Committee. Shortlisted candidates will be invited to interview. Only applicants selected for the short list will be contacted. Wilfrid Laurier University endeavors to fill positions with qualified candidates who have a combination of education, experience, skills and abilities to successfully perform the duties of the position while demonstrating Laurier's Employee Success Factors. Equity, diversity and creating a culture of inclusion are part of Laurier’s core values and central to the Laurier Strategy. Laurier is committed to increasing the diversity of faculty and staff and welcomes applications from candidates who identify as Indigenous, racialized, having disabilities, and from persons of any minority sexual and gender identities. Indigenous candidates who would like to learn more about equity and inclusive programing at Laurier are welcomed to contact the Office of Indigenous Initiatives. Candidates from other equity deserving groups who would like to learn more about equity and inclusive programing at Laurier are welcomed to contact Equity & Accessibility. We have strived to make our application process accessible, however if you require any assistance applying for a position or would like this job posting in an alternative format, please contact People and Culture. Contact information can be found at careers.wlu.ca/content/How-to-apply/ Should you be interested in learning more about this opportunity, please visit www.wlu.ca/careers for additional information and the online application system. All applications must be submitted online. Please note, a CV and letter of introduction will be required in electronic form. Job Segment: Computer Science, Information Systems, QC, Engineer, Technology, Research, Quality, Engineering Apply now » Find similar jobs: All jobs, Academic Positions Careers Home Frequently Searched Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © Copyright 2020 All Rights Reserved"
